  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA deepening upper low is progged to track southeastward across \nMichigan today. A shortwave rotating around the low, and a\nreinforcing surface cold front, will approach and cross the\nUpper Ohio Valley region today, with scattered showers expected.\nThe coverage of the showers should be more numerous north of\nPIT, where more favorable lift and moisture is expected. \n\nA decrease in the showers is expected tonight as the initial\nshortwave and reinforcing front exit. Expect showers to increase\nin coverage again on Saturday as the upper low tracks across the\nLower Great Lakes and Upper Ohio Valley region. The boundary\nlayer flow should stay swrly through early Saturday morning,\nbefore shifting to the WNW through the day. Temperatures at 850\nmb are expected to range from -1 to -3 deg C, with a lake\ntemperature around +17 deg C. Some lake enhancement to the\nshowers is expected under the cold WNW flow. There could be a\nfew snowflakes that mix in Saturday morning north of PIT, and at\ntimes Saturday in the higher terrain, though critical\nthicknesses and boundary layer temperatures indicate conditions\nwill be warm enough for rain across the remainder of the area.  \n\nThe upper low is expected to move east of the area by Saturday\nnight, with shortwave ridging building in across the region\nending the showers. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nModel ensembles indicate a broad upper trough will persist\nacross the NE CONUS from Sunday through much of the upcoming\nweek. A shortwave tracking through the trough could bring a few\nshowers north of I-80 Sunday night, though much of the rest of\nthe period should be dry as high pressure builds under the\ntrough. The trough is progged to deepen later in the week, with\nthe pattern becoming more active. Rain, and possibly some snow\nchances should increase late in the week as the trough deepens,\nwith some lake enhancement also possible.\n\nTemperatures should average near seasonable levels for much of\nthe week.",
